  "question": "If $|P| = |Q| = 1$, then prove that $adj(Q^{-1}P^{-1}) = PQ$.",
  "answeroption": "C",
  "explanation": "We know that: $adj(A) = |A| \\cdot A^{-1}$<br /><br />So: $adj(Q^{-1}P^{-1}) = |Q^{-1}P^{-1}| \\cdot (Q^{-1}P^{-1})^{-1}$<br /><br />First: $|Q^{-1}P^{-1}| = |Q^{-1}| \\cdot |P^{-1}| = \\frac{1}{|Q|} \\cdot \\frac{1}{|P|} = 1 \\cdot 1 = 1$<br /><br />Second: $(Q^{-1}P^{-1})^{-1} = (P^{-1})^{-1}(Q^{-1})^{-1} = PQ$<br /><br />Therefore: $adj(Q^{-1}P^{-1}) = 1 \\cdot PQ = PQ$",
